                        "review": "[h1] In A Nutshell [/h1]\n\n🔵 [b] Pros [/b]\n+ Retro, but also pleasant and clean style, inspired to early 2000s open-world adventure games.\n+ Quests are varied enough to not fall into repetition and tedium, keeping the experience interesting.\n+ Decent variety of different enemies and bosses to face in combat.  \n+ Good pacing of progression, based mostly on equip and skills rather than levels, throughout the adventure.\n+ Overall decent storyline, with some good quality comic-style narrative panels.\n+ Crafting / gathering mechanics are a welcome \"modern\" addition to an otherwise classic formula.\n\n🔴 [b] Cons [/b]\n- Limited variety of character equipment and spells to find, several of which aren't even worth using most of the time.\n- Problems with balance especially later on, when players can achieve insane stats such as 100% critical chance and unlimited MP with certain equip combinations, resulting in less challenge.\n- Moderate duration: it takes 6-8 hours to explore everything, complete most quests and get all the best equip in the game. \n\n\n[b]Tanzia[/b] is one of those titles trying to deliver the graphics, feel and elements of adventure games coming from two decades or so ago, into modern times and format. By all means, this is not a bad purpose, especially for nostalgics of those times, such as yours truly. \n\nThis game proposes a zone-based open-world gameplay, with a large map available overall, but many of its areas only opening after fulfilling appropriate requirements, such as having an item on the character, or completing a specific quest. Zones are leveled and contain enemies balanced to what the player level should be when entering said areas. In this Tanzia does a good job, never putting players against too strong (or feeble) enemies. NPCs in the various areas will both deliver information about the world, game story, and lore, while also give out mostly one quest each, ranging from the regular fetch, elimination, or delivery quests, to more unique types of assignments.\n\nEquipping the character here is more important than leveling up, since that results just in a Max HP boost and little else, while equipment such as staves, armor and magical gems provide large bonuses to damage resistance, maximum mana pool, and a variety of other attributes such as crit. chance, better loot chance, and so on. This gives players the possibility of partially building a character suiting their own play-style. Potions are another important element, which can be bought, looted, or crafted with found materials, granting a variety of positive effects.\n\nThis all being said, Tanzia falls short on mostly two things, the first being the limited variety of equipment a player can find for the character: a handful (4-5) unique gems, nine spells, and just 3 staff types and 2 armors, 1 only type of boots. The second thing is balance, as all gems giving bonuses stack together, and so do potions, resulting in combinations granting insane stats especially dps-wise, allowing players to 1-shot entire mobs of enemies with a critical hit and some AoE spells.\n\n[u]Overall, Tanzia is a good open world, old school adventure game, resulting in a pleasant experience from start to finish, never getting tedious or boring. Its elements are designed well enough in most part, and the good sides overcome the few bad ones by a large margin. nostalgics and action game fans should definitely give this a try.[/u]\n\n[quote]Liked this review, and want to see more of the same quality? Check out [url=https://store.steampowered.com/curator/39323490]Tamaster's Review Archive[/url] to see all my Reviews in a single, convenient location.[/quote]\n\n[h1] In-depth Feature Analysis [/h1]\n\n\n🎮 [b] Gameplay [/b]\nThe gameplay of Tanzia is divided into Exploration, Crafting and Combat.\n\n[u]Exploration[/u] is performed in third-person, moving mostly on foot throughout the island, encountering enemies, friendly NPCs, finding hidden loot chests and other types of secrets in the process. After some time on the main and some side quests, two forms of fast travel will be unlocked, one using static waypoints as teleports, while another using a consumable to return at the starting village every time. With the presence of cheap speed potions, moving around is not much tedious and rather fast, also an item later on can increase movement speed permanently. While exploring, gathering all kinds of raw materials is a good idea to craft potions when near an alchemy table.\n\n[u]Crafting[/u] is done via some workbenches. Alchemy Worktable will enable players to create many potions, provided the recipe was acquired beforehand, granting one-time effects such as health passive regen, better movement speed, restoring mana instantly, or even temporary damage immunity. The better an effect is, the harder the potion recipe will be, requiring rare materials. Gemcrafting simply allows to socket up to three (later four) gems in the magic staff, granting powerful effects, some of which unique and rare. \n\n\n📖 [b] Story [/b]\nThe story of Tanzia is sufficiently elaborate and well-written to keep players interested, even if there is a lack of character development personality-wise, while the whole atmosphere has not enough seriousness to it.\n\n👊 [b] Combat System [/b]\nCombat in Tanzia is done in an action-rpg style, where dodging enemy attacks and generally never standing still are fundamentals. there is no Stamina, only HP and Mana, like in old-school games, so kiting enemies around while casting spells will be anyone's bread and butter. As a Shaman, the main offensive will be done with spells, some AoE, some single target, and some utility ones. In the end though, AoE damage spells are by far the best ones for mowing down hordes of foes, which is what players will do most of the time. Single target ones are decent vs. bosses. Utility spells are pretty much useless altogether. Melee is an option, though very low damage and just a last resort. It's surprisingly good to stagger bosses with multiple crits tho.\n\nCombat is okay until a point, where players can stack together 50% crit chance gems (or just 30% is enough) and get insane stats such as 150% crit chance (!), resulting in enemies being critted every time by AoE spells, decimating dozens of foes instantly.\n\n\n💰 [b] Content & Pricing [/b]\nPriced at 17€ base price, offering 6-8 hours of content with not much replay value, I recommend waiting for at least a 25% sale before buying to all but the most fanatic players of this genre of games. \n\n🔗 [b] Balance & Challenge [/b]\nBalance is offset towards the player's side after some point, resulting in challenge being severely downgraded even on the hardest difficulty. If somehow players choose to not make one of these OP builds, then challenge level results balanced-\n\n\n[h1] Technical Analysis [/h1]\n\n\n🎥 [b] Graphics [/b]\nPS2-era graphics, as intended by the devs, give out an old-school feeling to the experience, but are not bad to look at by any means. Just do not expect anything fancy or modern into them.\n\n🔊 [b] Sound [/b]\nDecent soundtrack and average sound effects, while average narration quality in cutscenes. \n\n🔩 [b] Performance & Stability [/b]\nThe game runs flawlessly in 4K 60FPS all maxed out. No issues. [GTX1080, 16GB RAM, i7 4820K]\n\n💻 [b] Artificial Intelligence [/b]\nEnemy AI consists, as expected, in a follow and attack pattern, while some bosses have more elaborate behavior patterns at times. Friendly NPCs will not help you in combat at any time.\n\n🏠 [b] Quality of Life [/b]\nMenus, UI and generally interface do the job and present no particular issues.\n\n🐛 [b] Bugs & Issues [/b]\nNo major bugs to report.\n\n\n[h1] Conclusion [/h1]\nTanzia is definitely a good game, flawed by some balance issues and not a long duration time for the price. It's not perfect, but surely a good quality product at an Indie level, and players liking the genre will definitely have fun.\n\n\nThis product was reviewed using a free key provided by the Developers.",
